WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.687
Good morning, and thank you.

2
00:00:01.687 --> 00:00:03.250
I want to make one proposal.

3
00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.097
Based on current visibility,
many agencies do not operate

4
00:00:07.097 --> 00:00:11.025
a comprehensive enterprise
HR-driven identity lifecycle.

5
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:13.012
In the processes I have observed,

6
00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:18.162
many workforce identity changes in Active
Directory and Entra ID, and the access and

7
00:00:18.162 --> 00:00:23.312
credentials that hang off them, remain
dependent on tickets or manual coordination.

8
00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:25.945
Two changes are coming regardless:

9
00:00:25.945 --> 00:00:30.757
Federal HR 2.0 moves each agency's
HR onto Oracle Core HCM in waves,

10
00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:34.934
and current planning assumes
onboarding through 2027;

11
00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:39.837
agency identity is moving from
Active Directory toward Entra ID.

12
00:00:39.837 --> 00:00:44.609
My proposal is to use the two together
to start HR-driven identity — HR,

13
00:00:44.609 --> 00:00:47.335
through OPM's standard personnel data,

14
00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:52.874
as the source of authority for Entra ID,
Active Directory and the rest of ICAM.

15
00:00:52.874 --> 00:00:56.613
That's Microsoft's own term:
HR-driven provisioning.

16
00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.392
The thesis in one slide.

17
00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:04.400
On the left, two migrations that happen
regardless: HR moves to Core HCM,

18
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.543
and identity moves from Active
Directory toward Entra ID.

19
00:01:08.543 --> 00:01:13.717
Meanwhile, the enterprise lifecycle remains
substantially manual and fragmented.

20
00:01:13.717 --> 00:01:16.329
In the middle, what OPM leaves to us.

21
00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:20.098
OPM fixes the HR side; how
the agency's directories

22
00:01:20.098 --> 00:01:24.142
and ICAM consume that data
is the agency's decision.

23
00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:28.346
And the solicitation requires
SCIM 2.0 provisioning —

24
00:01:28.346 --> 00:01:30.830
exactly what Entra ID consumes.

25
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:34.627
On the right, the proposal:
start HR-driven identity,

26
00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:40.142
with HR as the source of authority,
expressed in OPM's standard attributes.

27
00:01:40.142 --> 00:01:45.021
So the real choice is: carry ticket-driven
identity through both migrations,

28
00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:50.431
or start HR-driven identity once, while
both platforms are being rebuilt anyway.

29
00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.480
Why we need HR-driven ICAM
at all — and why now.

30
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.162
Zero Trust is federal
strategy — OMB M-22-09,

31
00:01:58.162 --> 00:02:06.343
with NIST SP 800-207 as the architecture
and CISA's maturity model as the yardstick.

32
00:02:06.343 --> 00:02:11.316
SASE is one way these controls can be
delivered near users and cloud resources,

33
00:02:11.316 --> 00:02:16.817
which TIC 3.0 opens up for agencies,
instead of only at our network perimeter.

34
00:02:16.817 --> 00:02:21.489
Reliable ICAM supplies critical
identity signals to those decisions:

35
00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:25.701
the user presents a token from a
PIV card or phishing-resistant MFA,

36
00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:29.300
and Entra ID decides access
through Conditional Access

37
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.517
and dynamic groups that
read the user's attributes.

38
00:02:32.517 --> 00:02:36.792
In the perimeter era, a wrong department
meant a wrong phone-book entry.

39
00:02:36.792 --> 00:02:40.238
Now it means the wrong groups,
and so the wrong access —

40
00:02:40.238 --> 00:02:42.642
and a stale status is worse than ever.

41
00:02:42.642 --> 00:02:45.772
In the perimeter era an
orphaned account was already a

42
00:02:45.772 --> 00:02:49.042
standing security violation
that could enable a breach.

43
00:02:49.042 --> 00:02:51.842
Now it's a valid token
for someone who has left,

44
00:02:51.842 --> 00:02:56.005
usable from anywhere, because there
is no perimeter left to stop it.

45
00:02:56.005 --> 00:02:58.929
When access decisions
depend on attributes,

46
00:02:58.929 --> 00:03:03.180
the accuracy of employee data
drives the accuracy of access.

47
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.852
Why this matters to everyone
who works in an agency.

48
00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:10.457
In many agencies Active
Directory feeds the phone book,

49
00:03:10.457 --> 00:03:13.277
and nothing feeds Active
Directory from HR.

50
00:03:13.277 --> 00:03:16.411
Titles, offices, phone
numbers and managers stay

51
00:03:16.411 --> 00:03:19.466
wrong until someone
notices and files a ticket.

52
00:03:19.466 --> 00:03:24.366
And every time someone changes management,
their organization, their manager

53
00:03:24.366 --> 00:03:29.040
and their access to tools have to be
updated by hand, after the fact, by ticket.

54
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.852
HR already knows all of it.

55
00:03:30.852 --> 00:03:35.854
With HR-driven IT, the personnel action
that records the reassignment updates the

56
00:03:35.854 --> 00:03:40.561
organization and manager in Entra ID and
Active Directory, and the phone book,

57
00:03:40.561 --> 00:03:45.048
the approval chain and tool access
follow from the authoritative source —

58
00:03:45.048 --> 00:03:49.540
instead of each person, and the help
desk, keeping them current by hand.

59
00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:51.200
The window.

60
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:59.175
The December 10, 2025 OMB/OPM memo directs
one government-wide Core HCM platform.

61
00:03:59.175 --> 00:04:04.563
Oracle Fusion Cloud HCM
was awarded June 11, 2026.

62
00:04:04.563 --> 00:04:08.225
First go-lives are January to March 2027.

63
00:04:08.225 --> 00:04:12.644
Current planning, based on
published Federal HR 2.0 material,

64
00:04:12.644 --> 00:04:15.913
has Wave 2 agencies
following in fiscal 2027;

65
00:04:15.913 --> 00:04:20.862
agency-specific milestones are
confirmed with the program office.

66
00:04:20.862 --> 00:04:25.795
Program material describes interconnections
occurring during the onboarding period,

67
00:04:25.795 --> 00:04:29.862
and for an agency not yet on the platform,
that onboarding still lies ahead.

68
00:04:29.862 --> 00:04:35.462
By fiscal 2028 EHRI and
eOPF are decommissioned.

69
00:04:35.462 --> 00:04:38.488
I'd describe that not as a
deadline but as a window.

70
00:04:38.488 --> 00:04:43.606
Inside it, the data standards, the
cleanup, the interconnection design — and,

71
00:04:43.606 --> 00:04:47.801
on our side, the Entra ID migration
plan — are all being decided,

72
00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:51.576
and an HR-driven identity feed
can be written into them.

73
00:04:51.576 --> 00:04:56.885
After it closes, both migrations finish with
ticket-driven identity carried forward,

74
00:04:56.885 --> 00:05:00.775
and adding HR-driven identity
later means reopening both.

75
00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:05.745
This is the heart of the proposal, and
it's the programmatic data knowledge.

76
00:05:05.745 --> 00:05:10.282
OPM already defines the personnel
data every agency records.

77
00:05:10.282 --> 00:05:14.432
Agency and subelement give the
legal employer and component.

78
00:05:14.432 --> 00:05:16.982
Duty station is the location of record.

79
00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:22.220
Series, pay plan and grade give a
position category for role-based access.

80
00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.807
Supervisory status tells us
who can approve and review.

81
00:05:25.807 --> 00:05:28.971
Tenure and appointment type
drive the worker type.

82
00:05:28.971 --> 00:05:32.241
And the nature-of-action
code, with its effective date,

83
00:05:32.241 --> 00:05:34.620
tells us what just happened — next slide.

84
00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.645
Why build on OPM's standard rather
than on Oracle's field names?

85
00:05:38.645 --> 00:05:42.503
Because OPM standards provide
a common federal vocabulary

86
00:05:42.503 --> 00:05:45.820
that agencies can map into
a shared canonical model.

87
00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.603
We map them to Entra ID and AD once,
for every employee population —

88
00:05:50.603 --> 00:05:54.845
and if the HR vendor ever
changes, the mapping still holds.

89
00:05:54.845 --> 00:05:59.170
One honest limit: HRIT covers
federal employees only.

90
00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:01.022
In the processes I have observed,

91
00:06:01.022 --> 00:06:04.383
contractors and vendors are
tracked in COR spreadsheets.

92
00:06:04.383 --> 00:06:07.272
A contractor authority
decision is required,

93
00:06:07.272 --> 00:06:10.466
and I propose evaluating
three candidate sources:

94
00:06:10.466 --> 00:06:13.584
Core HCM's Contingent Worker
type — a person record,

95
00:06:13.584 --> 00:06:16.930
an assignment and a lifecycle
much like an employee's,

96
00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.504
though with no vendor-management
layer; PIV sponsorship;

97
00:06:20.504 --> 00:06:23.014
and a contractor suitability register,

98
00:06:23.014 --> 00:06:27.957
which ties each contractor to an agency
contract with a suitability decision.

99
00:06:27.957 --> 00:06:31.070
That's a decision to put in
front of the program early.

100
00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.587
Personnel actions can
initiate the lifecycle,

101
00:06:34.587 --> 00:06:38.558
after their identity interpretations
and exceptions are approved.

102
00:06:38.558 --> 00:06:43.233
For federal employees, every personnel
action carries a nature-of-action code,

103
00:06:43.233 --> 00:06:48.557
and I propose mapping the families to
identity events: the 100 series are joiners,

104
00:06:48.557 --> 00:06:55.032
the 700 series movers, the 500 series
conversions, the 300 series separations.

105
00:06:55.032 --> 00:07:01.007
Contractors have no SF-50; their events
come from contract start and end dates.

106
00:07:01.007 --> 00:07:03.583
Two families matter more
than people expect.

107
00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:08.811
The 400 series — furlough, leave without
pay, suspension — is not a leaver;

108
00:07:08.811 --> 00:07:12.994
the person is still employed and
policy restricts the account.

109
00:07:12.994 --> 00:07:17.320
The 200 series, return to
duty, lifts those restrictions.

110
00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.978
Getting this wrong either
locks out people on

111
00:07:19.978 --> 00:07:22.845
leave or leaves access open
for people who've left.

112
00:07:22.845 --> 00:07:27.820
The codes also settle precedence — a
separation wins — and rescinded offers.

113
00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:32.724
HR, security, labor-relations, privacy,
and personnel-policy owners would

114
00:07:32.724 --> 00:07:38.745
validate the mappings, exceptions, precedence
rules, and actions before automation.

115
00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:42.833
Identifiers are where data
architecture earns its keep.

116
00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:47.244
A common design says the employee
ID is the person, for life.

117
00:07:47.244 --> 00:07:49.773
It breaks the moment the source changes,

118
00:07:49.773 --> 00:07:52.607
a contractor converts,
or someone is rehired.

119
00:07:52.607 --> 00:07:58.065
So I model identifiers in layers: an
enterprise person key the agency controls;

120
00:07:58.065 --> 00:08:03.101
a source key per HR system; an affiliation
key per employment or contract;

121
00:08:03.101 --> 00:08:08.558
a directory ID per account; and credential
identifiers per PIV or certificate.

122
00:08:08.558 --> 00:08:11.608
The person is the tracked
entity, not the account.

123
00:08:11.608 --> 00:08:15.594
That's what survives rehire,
conversion and reorganization.

124
00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:19.705
And many agencies already have a
strong candidate — for example,

125
00:08:19.705 --> 00:08:24.132
a Mainframe PIN that already serves
as the person ID and employee ID.

126
00:08:24.132 --> 00:08:27.871
I propose it for confirmation
as the enterprise person key;

127
00:08:27.871 --> 00:08:32.832
adoption requires validating its
uniqueness, persistence, non-reassignment,

128
00:08:32.832 --> 00:08:37.182
population coverage, privacy
constraints and historical behavior.

129
00:08:37.182 --> 00:08:42.192
Once it's confirmed, map Core HCM's
PersonNumber to it — so when the agency

130
00:08:42.192 --> 00:08:47.846
moves to Core HCM, only the source key
changes, and nothing downstream has to.

131
00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.498
Logical and physical modeling,
following the data from HR to ICAM.

132
00:08:53.498 --> 00:08:57.662
In the middle is the logical model:
a person with an enterprise key the

133
00:08:57.662 --> 00:09:02.410
agency controls — their assignments, and
the lifecycle events that change them.

134
00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:05.839
On the left, the OPM element
that feeds each field;

135
00:09:05.839 --> 00:09:10.641
then where it lands in Entra ID and
Active Directory; and on the right,

136
00:09:10.641 --> 00:09:16.042
what ICAM does with it — component scope,
location policy, role-based access,

137
00:09:16.042 --> 00:09:20.672
approvals, and the joiner, mover
and leaver actions, including PIV.

138
00:09:20.672 --> 00:09:26.283
Three physical rules: native attributes
first; codes, never display strings, in

139
00:09:26.283 --> 00:09:28.354
anything that grants access;

140
00:09:28.354 --> 00:09:32.884
and one governed crosswalk as
the only place mappings live.

141
00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.875
Logical architecture and
Microsoft realization.

142
00:09:36.875 --> 00:09:41.340
The top row is the architecture, and
it's stable: an authoritative source,

143
00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.897
a boundary transformation,
the governed identity model,

144
00:09:44.897 --> 00:09:48.075
a provisioning service,
and the targets and ICAM.

145
00:09:48.075 --> 00:09:54.123
The bottom row is how Microsoft realizes it
today: Oracle Core HCM, Azure Functions,

146
00:09:54.123 --> 00:10:00.348
the canonical model and provenance store,
Graph and the Entra provisioning service,

147
00:10:00.348 --> 00:10:03.550
then Entra ID, AD, Intune
and card management.

148
00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:07.162
Products in the bottom row can
change; the top row doesn't.

149
00:10:07.162 --> 00:10:10.263
The table is why the two
migrations belong together.

150
00:10:10.263 --> 00:10:15.900
Done separately, the Entra ID migration
moves ticket-created accounts as they are.

151
00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:21.463
Done together, each population arrives
in Entra ID already HR-sourced.

152
00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:24.195
ICAM — beyond accounts.

153
00:10:24.195 --> 00:10:28.095
Credentials: the joiner event
schedules PIV enrollment.

154
00:10:28.095 --> 00:10:31.775
The leaver event blocks sign-in
and revokes sessions first —

155
00:10:31.775 --> 00:10:33.652
Microsoft's documented order,

156
00:10:33.652 --> 00:10:38.684
and the step that closes certificate
sign-in immediately rather than at the next

157
00:10:38.684 --> 00:10:41.313
revocation-list refresh
— then revokes PIV,

158
00:10:41.313 --> 00:10:46.645
derived credentials and device certificates
in parallel, reconciled to completion.

159
00:10:46.645 --> 00:10:51.447
Assurance: record each person's proofing
level and authenticator assurance as

160
00:10:51.447 --> 00:10:55.522
governed attributes, treat missing
proofing evidence as a finding,

161
00:10:55.522 --> 00:10:59.233
and enforce phishing-resistant
MFA under M-22-09.

162
00:10:59.233 --> 00:11:03.714
Access: baseline access derived by
rule from the standard attributes;

163
00:11:03.714 --> 00:11:09.200
position attributes scoping candidates
for privileged eligibility, with explicit

164
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.286
approval and PIM still required;

165
00:11:11.286 --> 00:11:14.608
and any activity from a
separated person is an alert.

166
00:11:14.608 --> 00:11:18.189
The result: every entitlement
has a "because" — a rule,

167
00:11:18.189 --> 00:11:22.070
an attribute, and the
personnel action that set it.

168
00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:26.852
Privacy — which is where a data
architect earns trust with HR.

169
00:11:26.852 --> 00:11:32.028
Moving HR attributes into a directory
is a disclosure of personnel records.

170
00:11:32.028 --> 00:11:38.143
So: confirm the routine-use basis under
the governing system-of-records notices —

171
00:11:38.143 --> 00:11:41.293
OPM/GOVT-1 for general personnel records,

172
00:11:41.293 --> 00:11:44.628
plus the agency's own —
before anything flows.

173
00:11:44.628 --> 00:11:49.427
Minimize: send only attributes an
identity decision actually reads,

174
00:11:49.427 --> 00:11:53.890
as codes rather than narrative, and
never use the SSN as a join key.

175
00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:58.440
Update the privacy impact assessment
for the new flow and its consumers.

176
00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.935
And protect the flow: encrypt every leg,

177
00:12:00.935 --> 00:12:06.002
and keep logs to the person key and a
payload hash rather than the record itself.

178
00:12:06.002 --> 00:12:10.247
The principle underneath: HR
remains the system of records;

179
00:12:10.247 --> 00:12:15.916
identity is a governed consumer that
never writes an HR-mastered fact back.

180
00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:19.322
The governance I'd bring
to deliver it safely.

181
00:12:19.322 --> 00:12:23.986
The data foundation first: an authority
map with one owner per attribute,

182
00:12:23.986 --> 00:12:27.173
and an enterprise person
key the agency controls.

183
00:12:27.173 --> 00:12:29.722
A security assessment of the data flow.

184
00:12:29.722 --> 00:12:33.284
An acceptance gate with a signed
validation before cutover.

185
00:12:33.284 --> 00:12:36.348
And a cutover that proves
HR is the sole source for

186
00:12:36.348 --> 00:12:39.410
a population before the
ticket path closes for it.

187
00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.825
Then the operating
rules on the bottom row,

188
00:12:41.825 --> 00:12:44.447
from lifecycle rules
through change control.

189
00:12:44.447 --> 00:12:51.343
Together that produces evidence for AC-2,
PS-4, PS-5, IA-4 and SI-12 as a byproduct

190
00:12:51.343 --> 00:12:57.373
of operating — supporting those controls,
not claiming to close them on its own.

191
00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.720
One standard, shared across agencies.

192
00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:04.603
Participating civilian agencies
are moving toward shared

193
00:13:04.603 --> 00:13:08.720
Core HCM capabilities and common
federal personnel standards.

194
00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:14.174
If one agency defines HR-driven ICAM as
a standard — crosswalk, canonical model,

195
00:13:14.174 --> 00:13:15.992
identifiers and evidence —

196
00:13:15.992 --> 00:13:19.958
other agencies can reuse it
instead of building their own.

197
00:13:19.958 --> 00:13:24.190
And attributes that mean the same
thing everywhere make details,

198
00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:28.032
transfers, shared services and
cross-agency access easier.

199
00:13:28.032 --> 00:13:31.051
That's the work an enterprise
data architect does:

200
00:13:31.051 --> 00:13:34.291
working the issues with
other agencies and industry,

201
00:13:34.291 --> 00:13:37.309
developing options and
predicting their impact,

202
00:13:37.309 --> 00:13:42.021
and serving as the data expert on
interagency committees and working groups.

203
00:13:42.021 --> 00:13:46.870
The goal: offer the model for
interagency evaluation and reuse.

204
00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:49.395
The roadmap and the ask.

205
00:13:49.395 --> 00:13:52.726
Now: align with the
agency's HR 2.0 team so

206
00:13:52.726 --> 00:13:57.307
identity is a named consumer
of the interconnection.

207
00:13:57.307 --> 00:14:00.653
Before onboarding: the
crosswalk, authority map,

208
00:14:00.653 --> 00:14:03.246
contractor source and privacy basis.

209
00:14:03.246 --> 00:14:05.643
Then build and prove in shadow mode,

210
00:14:05.643 --> 00:14:10.770
cut each population over as it moves to
Entra ID, and sustain it with metrics.

211
00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:15.036
So the ask is simple: decide
now to start HR-driven identity,

212
00:14:15.036 --> 00:14:20.103
and build it into both the HR 2.0
onboarding and the Entra ID migration —

213
00:14:20.103 --> 00:14:24.458
so that Zero Trust and SASE
decide access on accurate data.

214
00:14:24.458 --> 00:14:29.838
My contribution as an enterprise data
architect: establish authority, identifiers,

215
00:14:29.838 --> 00:14:31.913
standards, lineage and evidence,

216
00:14:31.913 --> 00:14:35.757
so workforce events produce
measurable identity outcomes.

217
00:14:35.757 --> 00:14:38.645
Thank you — I look
forward to your questions.

218
00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:45.166
HR-driven identity is one piece of a larger
set of modernizations I've designed over

219
00:14:45.166 --> 00:14:48.066
the last several years:
Entra ID migration,

220
00:14:48.066 --> 00:14:51.297
next-generation DNS and
IP address management,

221
00:14:51.297 --> 00:14:54.611
field office WAN and
data-center consolidation,

222
00:14:54.611 --> 00:15:00.908
the TIC 3.0 Cloud and Branch Office cases,
the client-server to hybrid cloud path,

223
00:15:00.908 --> 00:15:05.713
SQL Server identity, the move from
Group Policy to Intune and Azure Arc,

224
